\chapter{Finding any MuLIB kernel in the source catalogue}
\label{ch:119}
\index{Finding any MuLIB kernel in the source catalogue}

The complete MuLIB source inventory lives in \OnxCodeTerm{MANIFEST.yaml}. Use it for families beyond the worked examples in \cref{ch:101} through \cref{ch:110}, including data structures, codecs, search, hardware and engineering, operating systems, graphics, network protocols, DSLs and publishing. Start with a capability or header name, then open the registered manual, implementation and test. Some reserved or bounded entries have narrower evidence than a general implementation.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
rg -n 'name: (cdd-plan|audio-resample|ipc-framed)' MANIFEST.yaml
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-exotract.py list-kernels
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-mulib.py plan ipc-framed
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Use the implementation kind to choose a compilation strategy, and follow dependency names recursively in the same manifest. Compare the build recipe with the header's required companion sources. Then check whether the On1x standard-library catalogue exposes a suitable wrapper and whether KernelBind lists a callable, discovery-only, or unsupported profile. This procedure covers kernels that have no dedicated chapter and continues to work as operators add or split families.

\section{Answer a catalogue question about audio resampling}
A reader asks whether ``audio-resample'' can convert two channels at
once and retain phase between buffers. Search the manifest for the
canonical hyphenated name. The row points to a static-inline header,
its manual, an audio-core dependency and a shared media fixture. Read
the purpose and limit together: the operation performs interleaved
linear conversion, but does not maintain a streaming phase history.
The shared fixture is a starting point for inspection, not evidence
that every possible rate and boundary was tested.

Check that interpretation against a tiny input. Use mono frames zero
and ten, a phase of zero, an input/output rate ratio of one half and
three requested output frames. The source positions are zero, one
half and one, giving output samples zero, five and ten. The ratio
is a step in \emph{source} frames per output frame. Reversing the ratio
would skip straight past the interpolated sample. With interleaved
stereo the position calculation is the same for both channels; the
output allocation needs twice as many floats, not twice as many frame
positions. The header requires disjoint, live input and output spans.

Now answer the phase question from the signature. It accepts a starting
phase by value and does not return a streaming state object. Calling
again with the same input and phase starts the calculation again.
Similarly the catalogue's normalized import alias makes the declaration
discoverable but supplies no representation for those caller-owned
output floats. A positive answer about channel count is compatible with
negative answers about both streaming state and ordinary source calls.

Request a fourth output frame in the mono exercise. Its source
position is one and a half, past the last input frame. The documented
endpoint rule repeats ten; it does not extrapolate a new sample of
fifteen.

